---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Swimming pool pump repair, step by step | GK Swimming Pools"
  description="Pool pump won't turn on, humming, or leaking? See the seven steps we follow for swimming pool pump repair in Santa Clarita and the San Fernando Valley."
  canonical="https://www.gkswimmingpools.com/blog/swimming-pool-pump-repair"
>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BlogPosting",
    "headline": "Swimming Pool Pump Repair: How We Fix a Broken Pool Pump, Step by Step",
    "description": "Pool pump won't turn on, humming, or leaking? See the seven steps we follow for swimming pool pump repair in Santa Clarita and the San Fernando Valley.",
    "datePublished": "2026-10-06",
    "author": { "@type": "LocalBusiness", "name": "GK Swimming Pools", "telephone": "(818) 804-8204" },
    "publisher": { "@type": "LocalBusiness", "name": "GK Swimming Pools", "url": "https://www.gkswimmingpools.com" },
    "image": "https://www.gkswimmingpools.com/images/services/pool-repair-equipment.webp",
    "mainEntityOfPage": { "@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://www.gkswimmingpools.com/blog/swimming-pool-pump-repair" }
  })} />

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why won't my pool pump turn on?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A pool pump that won't turn on usually has one of three causes: power isn't reaching the motor, the capacitor has failed, or the motor itself is worn out. Start by confirming the timer or automation is set to run. If it is and the pump still does nothing, the problem is electrical and should be diagnosed by a professional."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Should I repair or replace my pool pump?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Our rule of thumb is that a pump older than 6 to 8 years is usually smarter to replace. A repair on an old pump is a short term fix, because once one part wears out the others tend to follow. A new pump also comes with a warranty. On a newer pump, a small part like a capacitor or an o-ring is often all it takes."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I repair a pool pump myself?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "You can safely check the water level, empty the skimmer and pump baskets, inspect the lid o-ring, and confirm the timer is set to run. Stop there. Pool pumps run on high voltage and a capacitor can hold a charge even with the power off, so opening the motor or touching the wiring is a job for a professional."
        }
      }
    ]
  })} />

  <section class="blog-hero">
    <div class="blog-hero__bg">
      <img src="/images/services/pool-repair-equipment.webp"
        alt="Pentair pool pump taken apart into wet end and motor during a swimming pool pump repair"
        fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="800" height="600" />
    </div>
    <div class="blog-hero__overlay"></div>
    <div class="container blog-hero__inner">
      <span class="eyebrow">Pool Repair Guide</span>
      <h1>Swimming Pool Pump Repair: How We Fix a Broken Pool Pump, Step by Step</h1>
      <div class="blog-meta">
        <span>GK Swimming Pools</span>
        <span class="blog-meta__dot"></span>
        <span>5 min read</span>
        <span class="blog-meta__dot"></span>
        <span>October 6, 2026</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="blog-body">

        <div class="blog-intro">
          <p>Your pool pump is the heart of your pool. When it stops, the water stops moving, and a clear pool can turn green within days. The good news is that a broken pool pump doesn't always mean buying a new one. Here are the warning signs, what you can safely check yourself, and the exact steps we follow on a swimming pool pump repair call.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Warning Signs</span>
          <h2>Signs Your Pool Pump Needs Repair</h2>
          <p>Most pump problems show up in one of five ways. Knowing which one you have makes the repair faster.</p>
          <ul class="blog-check-list">
            <li><span><strong>It won't turn on.</strong> Power isn't reaching the motor, the capacitor has failed, or the motor is worn out.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>It hums but won't start.</strong> The motor is getting power but can't spin. A bad capacitor or a jammed impeller is the usual cause.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>It's leaking.</strong> Water under the pump usually points to a worn seal or o-ring.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>It's loud or screeching.</strong> That high-pitched sound is typically worn motor bearings.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>It won't prime.</strong> The pump runs but doesn't pull water, usually because air is getting in or water can't reach it.</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Check It Yourself</span>
          <h2>What You Can Safely Check Before You Call</h2>
          <p>Some pump problems have simple causes. Turn the pump off first, then check these four things.</p>
          <ul class="blog-check-list">
            <li><span><strong>Water level.</strong> It should sit about halfway up the skimmer opening. Any lower and the pump pulls in air.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>Baskets.</strong> Empty the skimmer basket and the pump basket. A packed basket starves the pump of water.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>Lid o-ring.</strong> Make sure the o-ring under the pump lid is clean, seated flat, and not cracked.</span></li>
            <li><span><strong>Timer.</strong> Confirm the timer or automation is actually set to run.</span></li>
          </ul>

          <div class="callout-warn">
            <p>Stop there. Don't open the motor or touch the wiring. Pool pumps run on high voltage, and a capacitor can hold a charge even with the power off. Anything electrical is a job for a professional.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Our Process</span>
          <h2>How We Handle a Swimming Pool Pump Repair</h2>
          <p>Every pump call follows the same seven steps. The order matters, because it keeps us from replacing a part that was never the problem.</p>

          <div class="step-list">
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">1</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Ask what the pump is doing</h3>
                <p>Silent, humming, loud, leaking, or not priming. Each symptom points to a different part of the pump, so your description tells us where to look first.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">2</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Shut off power at the breaker</h3>
                <p>Before anyone puts hands on the pump, the power goes off at the breaker. No exceptions.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">3</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Confirm power is reaching the pump</h3>
                <p>We check the timer or automation, inspect the wiring connections, and test the voltage at the motor. A pump that looks dead is sometimes a pump that isn't getting power.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">4</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Rule out the simple stuff</h3>
                <p>Pump basket, lid o-ring, water level, and air leaks. These cause a lot of priming problems, and they're quick to fix.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">5</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Open up the motor</h3>
                <p>We test the capacitor, check that the shaft spins freely, and look over the impeller, the seal, and the bearings.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">6</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Give you an honest quote</h3>
                <p>We weigh the pump's age and condition, then tell you plainly whether a repair or a replacement makes more sense.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="step-item">
              <div class="step-item__num">7</div>
              <div class="step-item__body">
                <h3>Repair it and test it</h3>
                <p>We make the repair, put the pump back together, prime it, and run it. We don't leave until it's moving water and there are no leaks.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Common Fixes</span>
          <h2>The Pool Pump Repairs We Do Most</h2>
          <p>Four fixes cover most of the pump calls we get.</p>
          <p><strong>Capacitor.</strong> The capacitor gives the motor the jolt it needs to start. When it fails, the pump hums or does nothing at all.</p>
          <p><strong>O-ring.</strong> O-rings dry out and crack over time. A bad one lets air in or water out, which leads to priming problems and leaks.</p>
          <p><strong>Motor.</strong> When the motor itself has worn out, we replace it.</p>
          <p><strong>Rewiring.</strong> Years of sun and heat take a toll on wiring and connections. Sometimes the pump is fine and the wiring feeding it is the problem.</p>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Repair or Replace</span>
          <h2>When Replacing the Pump Is the Smarter Move</h2>
          <p>Our rule of thumb is simple. If a pump is older than 6 to 8 years, replacing it is usually smarter than repairing it.</p>
          <p>A repair on an old pump is a short term fix. Once one part wears out, the others are rarely far behind, so you pay for a repair and then another piece fails. A new pump comes with a warranty. A repaired old pump doesn't.</p>
          <p>On a newer pump, a small part like a capacitor or an o-ring is often all it takes.</p>

          <div class="blog-tip">
            <div class="blog-tip__label">From a Recent Job</div>
            <p>A client's pump wouldn't turn on, and they wanted to know what a repair would cost. A repair was clearly cheaper than a new pump. Once we opened it up, we found two problems. The capacitor was failing, and the motor had two years left at best. We didn't charge for the repair attempt, so that money could go toward a new pump instead.</p>
          </div>

          <p>When a replacement is the right call, we install Pentair, Jandy, and Hayward. Those are the only three brands we use.</p>
        </div>

        <hr class="blog-divider"/>

        <div class="blog-section">
          <span class="eyebrow">Next Steps</span>
          <h2>Need a Pool Pump Looked At?</h2>
          <p>If your pool pump is acting up, give GK Swimming Pools a call. We're a family-run company, three generations in, and we handle swimming pool repair throughout the San Fernando Valley and Santa Clarita. We'll tell you what's wrong and whether a repair or a replacement makes more sense for your pump.</p>

          <div class="blog-cta-inline">
            <a href="/services/pool-repairs" class="btn btn--primary">Pool Repair Services</a>
            <a href="tel:8188048204" class="btn btn--outline">(818) 804-8204</a>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <div class="cta-band">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Pool Pump Giving You Trouble?</h2>
      <p>We'll diagnose it and give you an honest answer on repair versus replacement. Serving Santa Clarita and the San Fernando Valley.</p>
      <a href="/contact" class="btn btn--primary">Get In Touch</a>
    </div>
  </div>

</Layout>

<style>
  .blog-hero { position:relative; height:460px; overflow:hidden; display:flex; align-items:flex-end; }
  .blog-hero__bg { position:absolute; inset:0; }
  .blog-hero__bg img { width:100%; height:100%; object-fit:cover; object-position:center 40%; display:block; }
  .blog-hero__overlay { position:absolute; inset:0; background:linear-gradient(to top, rgba(6,47,97,.90) 0%, rgba(6,47,97,.35) 60%, rgba(6,47,97,.12) 100%); }
  .blog-hero__inner { position:relative; z-index:2; padding-bottom: var(--space-12); }
  .blog-hero__inner .eyebrow { color:var(--color-aqua); margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-hero__inner h1 { color:var(--color-white); font-size:clamp(1.7rem,3.6vw,2.6rem); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); max-width:820px; }
  .blog-meta { display:flex; align-items:center; gap: var(--space-3); color:rgba(255,255,255,.72); font-size:var(--text-sm); font-weight:600; flex-wrap:wrap; }
  .blog-meta__dot { width:4px; height:4px; border-radius:50%; background:var(--color-aqua); }

  .blog-body { max-width:760px; margin:0 auto; }
  .blog-intro { background:var(--color-mist); border-left:4px solid var(--color-aqua); padding:var(--space-6) var(--space-8); margin-bottom:var(--space-12); border-radius:0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0; }
  .blog-intro p { font-size:var(--text-lg); color:var(--color-deep); font-weight:600; line-height:1.7; margin:0; }
  .blog-section { margin-bottom:var(--space-12); }
  .blog-section .eyebrow { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-section h2 { margin-bottom: var(--space-6); }
  .blog-section p { color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.85; margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .blog-section strong { color:var(--color-deep); }
  .blog-check-list { list-style:none; margin: var(--space-4) 0; display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .blog-check-list li { display:flex; align-items:flex-start; gap: var(--space-3); font-size:var(--text-base); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.7; }
  .blog-check-list li::before { content:"\2713"; color:var(--color-aqua); font-weight:800; font-size:1rem; flex-shrink:0; margin-top:2px; }
  .blog-divider { border:none; border-top:2px solid var(--color-border); margin:var(--space-12) 0; }

  /* REPAIR STEPS */
  .step-list { display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:var(--space-3); margin:var(--space-6) 0; }
  .step-item {
    display:flex; gap:var(--space-4); align-items:flex-start;
    background:var(--color-white); border:1.5px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius:var(--radius-sm); padding:var(--space-4) var(--space-6);
  }
  .step-item__num {
    flex-shrink:0; width:38px; height:38px; border-radius:50%;
    background:var(--color-aqua); color:var(--color-deep);
    font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:800; font-size:1.05rem;
    display:flex; align-items:center; justify-content:center; line-height:1;
  }
  .step-item__body h3 {
    font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:700; font-size:var(--text-base);
    color:var(--color-deep); text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.03em;
    margin-bottom:var(--space-2);
  }
  .step-item__body p { font-size:var(--text-sm); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.7; margin:0; }

  /* WARNING CALLOUT */
  .callout-warn {
    background:var(--color-white); border:1.5px solid var(--color-border);
    border-left:3px solid #e8a33d; border-radius:var(--radius-sm);
    padding:var(--space-6); margin:var(--space-6) 0;
  }
  .callout-warn p { font-size:var(--text-sm); color:var(--color-muted); line-height:1.8; margin:0; }

  .blog-tip { background:var(--color-deep); border-radius:var(--radius-sm); padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-8); margin: var(--space-8) 0; }
  .blog-tip__label { font-size:var(--text-xs); font-weight:700; letter-spacing:.15em; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--color-aqua); margin-bottom: var(--space-2); font-family:var(--font-heading); }
  .blog-tip p { color:rgba(255,255,255,.88); margin:0; }

  .blog-cta-inline { display:flex; gap: var(--space-4); flex-wrap:wrap; margin-top: var(--space-8); }
  .btn--outline { display:inline-block; border:2px solid var(--color-deep); color:var(--color-deep); font-family:var(--font-heading); font-weight:700; font-size:var(--text-sm); letter-spacing:.06em; text-transform:uppercase; padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-8); border-radius:var(--radius-sm); text-decoration:none; }

  @media (max-width:640px) {
    .blog-hero { height:360px; }
    .blog-intro { padding: var(--space-4); }
    .step-item { gap:var(--space-4); padding:var(--space-4); }
    .blog-cta-inline { flex-direction:column; }
  }
</style>
